1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,803
A sailor, a pilot and a map maker
all draw the same simple thing.

2
00:00:04,803 --> 00:00:05,703
An arrow.

3
00:00:06,007 --> 00:00:08,258
Why an arrow, and not just a number?

4
00:00:08,708 --> 00:00:10,572
Aarav found out the hard way.

5
00:00:10,922 --> 00:00:15,350
A friend told him the library is two
kilometres from the college gate.

6
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:17,158
So he rode two kilometres.

7
00:00:17,158 --> 00:00:18,058
No library.

8
00:00:18,457 --> 00:00:21,238
He rode the right distance,
in the wrong direction.

9
00:00:21,238 --> 00:00:24,067
Let's learn the one drawing
that would have saved him.

10
00:00:24,717 --> 00:00:26,742
A vector is drawn as an arrow.

11
00:00:27,092 --> 00:00:30,688
The length of the arrow shows
its size, called the magnitude.

12
00:00:31,088 --> 00:00:33,060
The arrowhead shows its direction.

13
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:35,625
The starting end is called the tail.

14
00:00:35,625 --> 00:00:37,746
The pointed end is called the head.

15
00:00:38,396 --> 00:00:40,747
Let's draw one on our motion notebook.

16
00:00:41,097 --> 00:00:42,746
First, choose a scale.

17
00:00:42,746 --> 00:00:45,847
One centimetre on paper
stands for one kilometre.

18
00:00:46,247 --> 00:00:48,646
The library is two kilometres away.

19
00:00:48,646 --> 00:00:51,259
So the arrow grows to two centimetres.

20
00:00:51,659 --> 00:00:52,880
Now the direction.

21
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:56,177
We measure the angle from
east, turning towards north.

22
00:00:56,577 --> 00:00:58,218
Watch it in slow motion.

23
00:00:58,218 --> 00:01:01,929
The arrow turns to thirty
degrees north of east, and stops.

24
00:01:02,329 --> 00:01:04,427
Now stretch it to four kilometres.

25
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:07,977
The arrow doubles in length,
but the angle stays the same.

26
00:01:08,477 --> 00:01:12,752
On the graph, paper length grows in
a straight line with the real size.

27
00:01:12,752 --> 00:01:14,746
That straight line is the scale.

28
00:01:15,396 --> 00:01:17,756
So why did Aarav miss the library?

29
00:01:17,756 --> 00:01:19,031
Follow the links.

30
00:01:19,381 --> 00:01:22,111
Two kilometres is only the magnitude.

31
00:01:22,511 --> 00:01:26,457
Every point on this circle is
exactly two kilometres from the gate.

32
00:01:26,857 --> 00:01:29,917
So one number leaves a
whole circle of places.

33
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:32,877
Add the angle, and only one point is left.

34
00:01:32,877 --> 00:01:34,353
That is the library.

35
00:01:35,003 --> 00:01:37,355
Now the rules, one step at a time.

36
00:01:37,705 --> 00:01:38,605
Step one.

37
00:01:38,518 --> 00:01:42,148
In print, a vector is
written in bold, like bold A.

38
00:01:42,148 --> 00:01:44,915
By hand, we draw a small arrow on top.

39
00:01:45,665 --> 00:01:46,565
Step two.

40
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:51,817
Its magnitude is written between two
bars, mod A, or as A in plain type.

41
00:01:51,817 --> 00:01:54,003
A magnitude is never negative.

42
00:01:54,753 --> 00:01:55,736
Step three.

43
00:01:55,736 --> 00:01:59,239
Arrow length equals magnitude
divided by the scale.

44
00:01:59,239 --> 00:02:03,992
Two kilometres, at one kilometre
per centimetre, is two centimetres.

45
00:02:04,742 --> 00:02:05,642
Step four.

46
00:02:05,584 --> 00:02:08,176
In a plane, two numbers fix a vector.

47
00:02:08,176 --> 00:02:11,658
Its magnitude, and its angle
theta from a fixed line.

48
00:02:12,408 --> 00:02:13,403
Remember this.

49
00:02:13,403 --> 00:02:15,756
The length of the arrow is the size.

50
00:02:15,756 --> 00:02:17,708
The arrowhead is the direction.

51
00:02:18,408 --> 00:02:19,551
Side by side.

52
00:02:19,551 --> 00:02:20,969
Here is vector A.

53
00:02:20,969 --> 00:02:23,287
Three kilometres, pointing east.

54
00:02:23,687 --> 00:02:25,118
And here is minus A.

55
00:02:25,118 --> 00:02:28,349
The same length, but the
head points the other way.

56
00:02:28,349 --> 00:02:29,249
West.

57
00:02:29,466 --> 00:02:30,559
Now you try.

58
00:02:30,559 --> 00:02:33,348
A and minus A have the same magnitude.

59
00:02:33,348 --> 00:02:35,088
Are they equal vectors?

60
00:02:35,088 --> 00:02:36,445
Pause and decide.

61
00:02:39,645 --> 00:02:40,545
No!

62
00:02:40,137 --> 00:02:44,036
Equal vectors need the same
size and the same direction.

63
00:02:44,036 --> 00:02:46,398
Minus A points the opposite way.

64
00:02:47,048 --> 00:02:48,416
Here is a surprise.

65
00:02:48,416 --> 00:02:51,541
Slide an arrow across the
page without turning it.

66
00:02:51,941 --> 00:02:53,623
Its length did not change.

67
00:02:53,623 --> 00:02:55,442
Its direction did not change.

68
00:02:55,442 --> 00:02:57,461
So it is still the same vector.

69
00:02:57,861 --> 00:03:01,077
But turn it, even a little,
and you get a new vector.

70
00:03:01,077 --> 00:03:03,073
Where you draw it does not matter.

71
00:03:03,073 --> 00:03:04,496
Length and direction do.

72
00:03:05,096 --> 00:03:05,996
Your turn.

73
00:03:05,993 --> 00:03:09,540
An arrow two centimetres
long stands for six newtons.

74
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:12,561
What does a five
centimetre arrow stand for?

75
00:03:12,561 --> 00:03:13,881
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:17,081 --> 00:03:18,317
Fifteen newtons!

77
00:03:18,317 --> 00:03:22,595
Six newtons for two centimetres
is three newtons per centimetre.

78
00:03:22,595 --> 00:03:24,655
Five times three is fifteen.

79
00:03:25,305 --> 00:03:27,041
Now, one worked example.

80
00:03:27,391 --> 00:03:29,880
One centimetre stands for five newtons.

81
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:34,033
Draw a force of twenty newtons,
at sixty degrees from the floor.

82
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:37,646
Arrow length equals
force divided by scale.

83
00:03:37,646 --> 00:03:40,758
Twenty divided by five
is four centimetres.

84
00:03:41,508 --> 00:03:45,386
Draw a line four centimetres long
from the tail, along the floor.

85
00:03:46,136 --> 00:03:48,089
Place a protractor at the tail.

86
00:03:48,089 --> 00:03:50,882
Turn the line sixty
degrees up from the floor.

87
00:03:51,632 --> 00:03:53,854
Put the arrowhead at the far end.

88
00:03:53,854 --> 00:03:56,153
Four centimetres, at sixty degrees.

89
00:03:56,153 --> 00:03:58,653
That is twenty newtons, drawn exactly.

90
00:03:59,303 --> 00:04:00,768
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:01,118 --> 00:04:04,709
Many students think that
minus A has a negative size.

92
00:04:05,059 --> 00:04:05,959
Wrong.

93
00:04:05,725 --> 00:04:07,964
A magnitude is never negative.

94
00:04:07,964 --> 00:04:10,453
Minus A has the same size as A.

95
00:04:10,453 --> 00:04:12,353
Only the direction flips.

96
00:04:13,003 --> 00:04:14,602
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:14,602 --> 00:04:19,600
As quick questions on notation,
magnitude, and equal or negative vectors,

98
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:22,014
and inside longer vector problems.

99
00:04:22,614 --> 00:04:23,888
Exam level now.

100
00:04:23,888 --> 00:04:28,799
On a scale of one centimetre to two
newtons, vector A is an arrow four

101
00:04:28,799 --> 00:04:33,441
point five centimetres long, pointing
thirty degrees north of east.

102
00:04:33,441 --> 00:04:35,265
Which describes minus A?

103
00:04:35,265 --> 00:04:36,593
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:41,993 --> 00:04:43,289
The answer is A.

105
00:04:43,289 --> 00:04:46,222
Nine newtons, thirty
degrees south of west.

106
00:04:46,722 --> 00:04:47,735
Size first.

107
00:04:47,735 --> 00:04:53,129
Four point five times two is nine
newtons, and minus A has the same size.

108
00:04:53,629 --> 00:04:54,779
Direction next.

109
00:04:54,779 --> 00:04:56,985
Minus A points exactly opposite.

110
00:04:56,985 --> 00:05:01,419
Thirty degrees north of east flips
to thirty degrees south of west.

111
00:05:01,919 --> 00:05:04,959
B has a negative size,
which is impossible.

112
00:05:04,959 --> 00:05:07,213
C flips only half the direction.

113
00:05:07,213 --> 00:05:08,733
D forgets the scale.

114
00:05:09,333 --> 00:05:10,793
Here is your recap.

115
00:05:10,793 --> 00:05:12,295
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:15,495 --> 00:05:18,493
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:05:18,893 --> 00:05:19,793
One.

118
00:05:19,391 --> 00:05:23,666
Why is two kilometres from the gate
not enough to find the library?

119
00:05:24,566 --> 00:05:25,466
Two.

120
00:05:25,099 --> 00:05:29,857
On a scale of one centimetre to ten
newtons, how long is the arrow for

121
00:05:29,857 --> 00:05:31,352
thirty five newtons?

122
00:05:32,252 --> 00:05:33,152
Three.

123
00:05:32,836 --> 00:05:35,651
Are A and minus A equal vectors, and why?

124
00:05:36,751 --> 00:05:39,191
Next time: two ropes pull one box.

125
00:05:39,191 --> 00:05:44,241
How do two arrows join into one, and
what does it mean to subtract an arrow?

126
00:05:44,241 --> 00:05:47,285
We find out in vector
addition and subtraction.
